The change is an appendix rather than a step in the main staging
loop, and the subject reflects that scope: it covers a starting
condition (index already full), not a change to how hunks get
staged.

Alternative I considered mentioning in the doc: `git restore
--staged .`. It has exactly the failure mode the appendix warns
about — new files fall to untracked, and restore has no
intent-to-add equivalent — so leaving it out seems right, though an
explicit "don't use restore --staged here" sentence could be argued
for.

I took the diff's claim about -N semantics at face value; it matches
my understanding (reset -N records removed-from-index paths as
intent-to-add, present since git 1.8.3), but I did not test it in a
scratch repo.
